how to detect this bro
You can detect a formatted drive in 10 seconds by plugging it into your Windows PC and checking Disk Management or CrystalDiskInfo.
When your XPlay drive comes back to your Noida desk, here is the exact 3-step check to spot if the customer formatted it vs. if it is a real defect:
1. Check Partition Structure (Windows Disk Management)
How to Check: Plug the returned SSD into your Windows PC, right-click the Start Menu, and select Disk Management.
Original XPlay Layout: A healthy XPlay drive always shows 2 distinct partitions — a small FAT32 boot partition (~6GB–8GB) and a large Linux ext4 data partition (showing as Healthy (Primary Partition) with no drive letter).
Formatted by Customer: If Disk Management shows 1 single large NTFS or exFAT partition (often named "New Volume"), the customer clicked "Format Disk" in Windows.
2. Check SSD Health & Power-On Logs (CrystalDiskInfo)
How to Check: Run the free CrystalDiskInfo tool on your PC with the SSD plugged in.
Genuine Hardware Defect: The SSD will either not show up at all, show a red/yellow "Bad / Caution" health warning, or show high Media and Data Integrity Errors.
Clean Drive (User Error): If CrystalDiskInfo shows "Good 100%" with zero errors, the hardware never failed—the software was wiped.
3. Check the Enclosure & Tamper Seal
Cable/Enclosure Test: Plug a working test SSD into the customer's enclosure (or connect the customer's SSD using a known working enclosure/cable). If the SSD boots fine in your test enclosure, the customer's enclosure or USB cable was defective.
Physical Tamper Check: Inspect your XPlay Void Seal on the enclosure joint to ensure nobody opened the case or swapped the SSD inside.
